\description{
Compute the required sample size for estimating a population mean
with a specified margin of error or coefficient of variation.
}
\details{
Two modes:
\itemize{
\item \strong{MOE mode}: \code{n = deff * z^2 * var / (moe^2 + deff * z^2 * var / N)}.
An \code{rmoe} target enters here as \code{moe = rmoe * abs(mu)}.
\item \strong{CV mode}: Computes \code{CVpop = sqrt(var) / abs(mu)}, then
\code{n = deff * CVpop^2 / (cv^2 + deff * CVpop^2 / N)}.
}

\code{deff} appears in the denominator as well as the numerator: it inflates
the variance the finite population correction is then applied to, rather
than scaling a size already corrected. The two coincide only at infinite
\code{N}. For \code{var = 100}, \code{moe = 2}, \code{N = 100} and \code{deff = 2}, inflating
afterwards would give 97.98 against the correct 65.76.
\subsection{Finite population correction}{

Setting \code{N} to a finite value reduces the required sample size when
the sampling fraction (n/N) is non-negligible (rule of thumb: matters
when n/N > 5 percent). Unlike \code{\link[=n_prop]{n_prop()}}, no \code{N/(N-1)} adjustment is
needed because \code{var} is already defined on \code{N-1} degrees of freedom.
See \code{\link[=n_prop]{n_prop()}} for a fuller explanation of FPC.

All methods use the normal (z) quantile. This is standard for survey
sampling where the sample size is large enough for the CLT to apply.
}

\subsection{Sample size for a total}{

A separate \code{n_total()} function is not needed because the sample size
for a population total \eqn{\hat{Y} = N \bar{y}}{Yhat = N ybar} is identical to
the sample size for the mean. The two are related by a factor of
\eqn{N}:
\itemize{
\item \strong{CV mode}: \eqn{CV(\hat{Y}) = CV(\bar{y})}{CV(Yhat) = CV(ybar)}, so the required
sample size is the same. Use \code{n_mean(var, mu, cv)} directly.
\item \strong{MOE mode}: \eqn{MOE(\hat{Y}) = N \times MOE(\bar{y})}{MOE(Yhat) = N * MOE(ybar)}, so
divide the target MOE for the total by \eqn{N}:
\code{n_mean(var, moe = moe_total / N, N = N)}.
}

See Examples below.
}
